FILE items/def-cardinal-arithmetic.md
39:## Definition
40:
41:Let $\kappa$ and $\lambda$ be cardinals ([[def-cardinal]]), and recall the
42:notation of [[lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined]]:
43:
44:$$\kappa \sqcup \lambda = (\{0\} \times \kappa) \cup (\{1\} \times \lambda), \qquad {}^{\lambda}\kappa = \{\, h : h \text{ is a function } \lambda \to \kappa \,\}.$$
45:
46:**Sum and product.**
47:
48:$$\kappa \oplus \lambda \;:=\; \lvert \kappa \sqcup \lambda \rvert, \qquad \kappa \otimes \lambda \;:=\; \lvert \kappa \times \lambda \rvert .$$
49:
50:Both values exist in ZF and are cardinals: claim (c) of
51:[[lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined]] well-orders each of the two sets
52:explicitly, and [[lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set]] then supplies the
53:least equinumerous ordinal. **No choice principle is used.**
54:
55:**Exponentiation.**
56:
57:$$\kappa^{\lambda} \;:=\; \lvert {}^{\lambda}\kappa \rvert ,$$
58:
59:the number of functions from a set of size $\lambda$ to a set of size $\kappa$.
60:The right-hand side is defined exactly when ${}^{\lambda}\kappa$ is
61:well-orderable. **Assuming the Axiom of Choice** ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]) every
62:set is well-orderable ([[thm-well-ordering-theorem]]) and $\kappa^{\lambda}$ is
63:defined for all cardinals; every statement on this page that writes
64:$\kappa^{\lambda}$ for an infinite exponent says so in its own hypotheses.
65:
66:**Transport to arbitrary sets.** If $A$ and $B$ are well-orderable with
67:$\lvert A \rvert = \kappa$ and $\lvert B \rvert = \lambda$, then
68:
69:$$\lvert A \sqcup B \rvert = \kappa \oplus \lambda, \qquad \lvert A \times B \rvert = \kappa \otimes \lambda, \qquad \lvert {}^{B}A \rvert = \kappa^{\lambda}$$
70:
71:whenever the sets on the left have cardinalities at all, because $A \approx \kappa$
72:and $B \approx \lambda$ ([[def-equinumerous]]) and the three constructions
73:respect $\approx$ (claim (a) of [[lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined]],
74:[[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]). So the operations may be computed from
75:any representatives.
76:
77:**Finite and infinite cardinals.** A cardinal $\kappa$ is **finite** when
78:$\kappa \in \omega$ and **infinite** when $\omega \subseteq \kappa$, that is
79:$\omega \le \kappa$; by trichotomy ([[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]]) and
80:[[lem-omega-least-limit-ordinal]] every cardinal is exactly one of the two.
81:
FILE items/thm-cantor-powerset.md
42:## Statement
43:
44:Let $A$ be a set and $\mathcal{P}(A)$ its power set. Then there is **no
45:surjection** $A \to \mathcal{P}(A)$ ([[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]).
46:
47:Consequently $A \not\approx \mathcal{P}(A)$ while $A \preceq \mathcal{P}(A)$,
48:that is, $A \prec \mathcal{P}(A)$ ([[def-equinumerous]]): the power set is
49:strictly larger, for every set whatsoever.
50:
51:This is Cantor's diagonal argument in its non-circular form. It uses nothing
52:about $\mathbb{R}$, nothing about decimal or binary expansions, and no choice
53:principle: only the Power Set axiom, to form $\mathcal{P}(A)$, and Separation, to
54:form the diagonal set.
55:
FILE items/def-equinumerous.md
41:## Definition
42:
43:Let $A$ and $B$ be sets ([[def-injection-surjection-bijection]] for the
44:terminology).
45:
46:- $A$ and $B$ are **equinumerous**, written $A \approx B$, if there exists a
47:  bijection $f : A \to B$.
48:- $A$ is **dominated by** $B$, written $A \preceq B$, if there exists an
49:  injection $f : A \to B$.
50:- $A \prec B$ abbreviates: $A \preceq B$ and not $A \approx B$.
51:
FILE items/lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set.md
42:## Statement
43:
44:Call a set $X$ **well-orderable** when some relation well-orders it
45:([[def-well-order]]). Work in ZF, with no choice principle. Then:
46:
47:**(a)** $X$ is well-orderable if and only if $X \approx \alpha$
48:([[def-equinumerous]]) for some ordinal $\alpha$ ([[def-ordinal]]).
49:
50:**(b)** If $X$ is well-orderable there is a **least** ordinal equinumerous with
51:$X$. It is written $\lvert X \rvert$ and called the **cardinality** of $X$.
52:
53:**(c)** $\lvert X \rvert$ is a cardinal ([[def-cardinal]]).
54:
55:**(d)** If $X \approx Y$ and $X$ is well-orderable, then $Y$ is well-orderable
56:and $\lvert Y \rvert = \lvert X \rvert$.
57:
58:**(e)** $\lvert \alpha \rvert \le \alpha$ for every ordinal $\alpha$, and
59:$\lvert \alpha \rvert = \alpha$ exactly when $\alpha$ is a cardinal.
60:
61:Assuming the Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]) every set is
62:well-orderable ([[thm-well-ordering-theorem]]), so $\lvert X \rvert$ is then
63:defined for every set and is exactly the cardinality of [[def-cardinal]].
64:
65:**Why this item exists.** [[def-cardinal]] introduces $\lvert X \rvert$ under the
66:hypothesis "Assume the Axiom of Choice", and it needs that hypothesis only to
67:know that $X$ carries a well-order at all. Everything below is about
68:well-orderable sets and is a theorem of ZF, which is what makes it possible to
69:state Hessenberg's theorem and Tarski's theorem, one of which is choice-free and
70:the other of which is precisely about the gap between ZF and ZFC.
71:
FILE items/lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined.md
40:## Statement
41:
42:For sets $A$ and $B$ write
43:
44:$$A \sqcup B := (\{0\} \times A) \cup (\{1\} \times B), \qquad {}^{B}A := \{\, h : h \text{ is a function } B \to A \,\},$$
45:
46:so $A \sqcup B$ is the **disjoint union**, made disjoint by tagging, and
47:${}^{B}A$ is the set of all functions from $B$ to $A$. Work in ZF. Then:
48:
49:**(a) Representative independence.** If $A \approx A'$ and $B \approx B'$
50:([[def-equinumerous]]) then
51:
52:$$A \sqcup B \approx A' \sqcup B', \qquad A \times B \approx A' \times B', \qquad {}^{B}A \approx {}^{B'}A'.$$
53:
54:**(b) Power sets.** If $A \approx B$ then $\mathcal{P}(A) \approx \mathcal{P}(B)$.
55:
56:**(c) Two operations are choice-free.** For ordinals $\alpha$ and $\beta$
57:([[def-ordinal]]) the sets $\alpha \sqcup \beta$ and $\alpha \times \beta$ carry
58:**explicitly defined** well-orders ([[def-well-order]]), so each is equinumerous
59:with an ordinal and each has a cardinality
60:$\lvert \alpha \sqcup \beta \rvert$, $\lvert \alpha \times \beta \rvert$
61:in ZF ([[lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set]]).
62:
63:**(d) The third is not.** Nothing here well-orders ${}^{\beta}\alpha$, and no
64:argument on this page does. Assuming the Axiom of Choice
65:([[def-axiom-of-choice]]) every set is well-orderable
66:([[thm-well-ordering-theorem]]) and ${}^{\beta}\alpha$ has a cardinality like any
67:other set; that is where cardinal exponentiation gets its hypothesis.
68:
FILE items/def-injection-surjection-bijection.md
37:## Definition
38:
39:Let $A$ and $B$ be sets and let $f : A \to B$ be a function.
40:
41:- $f$ is **injective** (one-to-one) if $f(x) = f(y)$ implies $x = y$, for all
42:  $x, y \in A$.
43:- $f$ is **surjective** (onto) if for every $b \in B$ there is some $x \in A$
44:  with $f(x) = b$; equivalently, the image $f[A] := \{ f(x) : x \in A \}$ equals
45:  $B$.
46:- $f$ is **bijective** if it is both injective and surjective.
47:
48:For $S \subseteq A$ we write $f[S] = \{ f(x) : x \in S \}$ for the image of $S$,
49:and for $T \subseteq B$ we write $f^{-1}[T] = \{ x \in A : f(x) \in T \}$ for the
50:preimage of $T$.
51:
FILE items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md
40:## Statement
41:
42:Let $\kappa$, $\lambda$, $\mu$ be cardinals ([[def-cardinal]]) and let
43:$\oplus$, $\otimes$, $\kappa^{\lambda}$ be as in [[def-cardinal-arithmetic]].
44:Clauses (a) to (e) are theorems of ZF; the clauses naming an exponential hold
45:whenever those exponentials are defined, in particular under the Axiom of Choice
46:([[def-axiom-of-choice]]).
47:
48:**(a) Comparison.** $\kappa \le \lambda$ if and only if $\kappa \preceq \lambda$,
49:that is, if and only if there is an injection $\kappa \to \lambda$
50:([[def-equinumerous]]). More generally, if $A$ and $B$ are well-orderable and
51:$A \preceq B$ then $\lvert A \rvert \le \lvert B \rvert$.
52:
53:**(b) Commutativity and associativity.**
54:$\kappa \oplus \lambda = \lambda \oplus \kappa$,
55:$\kappa \otimes \lambda = \lambda \otimes \kappa$,
56:$(\kappa \oplus \lambda) \oplus \mu = \kappa \oplus (\lambda \oplus \mu)$ and
57:$(\kappa \otimes \lambda) \otimes \mu = \kappa \otimes (\lambda \otimes \mu)$.
58:
59:**(c) Distributivity.**
60:$\kappa \otimes (\lambda \oplus \mu) = (\kappa \otimes \lambda) \oplus (\kappa \otimes \mu)$.
61:
62:**(d) Units.** $\kappa \oplus 0 = \kappa$, $\kappa \otimes 0 = 0$,
63:$\kappa \otimes 1 = \kappa$, and $\kappa^{0} = 1$, $\kappa^{1} = \kappa$,
64:$1^{\kappa} = 1$, together with $0^{\kappa} = 0$ for $\kappa \ne 0$. The four
65:exponential unit laws need no choice principle, because the function sets they
66:count are empty, a singleton, or a copy of $\kappa$.
67:
68:**(e) Monotonicity.** If $\kappa \le \lambda$ then
69:$\kappa \oplus \mu \le \lambda \oplus \mu$ and
70:$\kappa \otimes \mu \le \lambda \otimes \mu$; and
71:$\kappa^{\mu} \le \lambda^{\mu}$, and $\mu^{\kappa} \le \mu^{\lambda}$ provided
72:$\mu \ne 0$.
73:
74:**(f) The two exponent laws.**
75:$\kappa^{\lambda \oplus \mu} = \kappa^{\lambda} \otimes \kappa^{\mu}$ and
76:$(\kappa^{\lambda})^{\mu} = \kappa^{\lambda \otimes \mu}$.
77:
78:**Each clause is an equality or an inequality of cardinals**, not merely of sizes: each side is
79:an ordinal, and the claim is that the two ordinals are the same.
80:
FILE items/def-cardinal.md
41:## Definition
42:
43:Write $X \approx Y$, and say $X$ and $Y$ are **equinumerous**, when there is a
44:bijection $X \to Y$.
45:
46:An ordinal $\kappa$ ([[def-ordinal]]) is a **cardinal**, equivalently an
47:**initial ordinal**, when
48:
49:$$\text{no } \alpha \in \kappa \text{ satisfies } \alpha \approx \kappa,$$
50:
51:that is, $\kappa$ is not equinumerous with any strictly smaller ordinal.
52:
53:**Cardinality, under the Axiom of Choice.** Assume the Axiom of Choice
54:([[def-axiom-of-choice]]) and let $X$ be a set. Then $X$ carries a well-order
55:([[thm-well-ordering-theorem]]), which has an order type $\alpha$
56:([[thm-mostowski-collapse]]) and in particular $\alpha \approx X$. Now
57:$\alpha^{+} = \alpha \cup \{\alpha\}$ is an ordinal ([[lem-ordinal-basics]],
58:claim (c)) whose elements are ordinals (claim (a)) and which contains $\alpha$,
59:so $C = \{\xi \in \alpha^{+} : \xi \approx X\}$ is a nonempty set of ordinals and
60:has an $\in$-least element $\kappa$ ([[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]]). This $\kappa$ is the
61:**cardinality** of $X$, written $|X|$; it is a cardinal, because
62:$\beta \in \kappa$ with $\beta \approx \kappa \approx X$ would lie in
63:$\alpha^{+}$ and contradict the minimality of $\kappa$.
64:
65:**Well-definedness: $\kappa$ does not depend on the well-order or on $\alpha$.**
66:The recipe above instantiates a well-order of $X$ and an order type $\alpha$ for
67:it, and $X$ will in general carry many well-orders with many different order
68:types, so the value $\kappa$ has to be shown independent of both. It is, because
69:$\kappa$ is in fact the least ordinal equinumerous with $X$ **outright**, a
70:description in which neither the well-order nor $\alpha$ appears. Let $\beta$ be
71:any ordinal with $\beta \approx X$. By trichotomy for ordinals
72:([[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]]) exactly one of $\beta \in \alpha^{+}$,
73:$\beta = \alpha^{+}$, $\alpha^{+} \in \beta$ holds. In the first case
74:$\beta \in C$, so $\kappa \subseteq \beta$ by minimality of $\kappa$. In the
75:other two cases claim (f) of [[lem-ordinal-basics]] gives
76:$\alpha^{+} \subseteq \beta$, and $\alpha \in \alpha^{+}$ because
77:$\alpha^{+} = \alpha \cup \{\alpha\}$, so $\alpha \in \beta$ and hence
78:$\alpha \subseteq \beta$ by claim (f) again; and $\alpha \in C$, so
79:$\kappa \subseteq \alpha$ by minimality, whence $\kappa \subseteq \beta$. In every case
80:$\kappa \subseteq \beta$, that is $\kappa \le \beta$. So $\kappa$ is the least
81:element of the collection of **all** ordinals equinumerous with $X$, and any two
82:runs of the recipe, from any two well-orders of $X$, return the same $\kappa$.
83:
FILE items/def-axiom-of-choice.md
41:## Definition
42:
43:The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.
44:
45:> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function
46:> ([[def-choice-function]]).
47:
48:Written out: for every set $\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,
49:there exists a function $g$ with domain $\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \in S$ for
50:all $S \in \mathcal{F}$.
51:
52:An equivalent formulation, immediate from the definition of the Cartesian
53:product, is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if $X_i \ne \emptyset$
54:for every $i \in I$, then $\prod_{i \in I} X_i \ne \emptyset$.
55:
FILE items/thm-well-ordering-theorem.md
46:## Statement
47:
48:Assume the Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Then **every set $X$ can
49:be well ordered**: there is a relation on $X$ making it a well-ordered set
50:([[def-well-order]]).
51:
52:The Axiom of Choice is used only inside [[thm-zorn]], and nowhere else in the
53:argument below.
54:
FILE items/lem-ordinal-trichotomy.md
40:## Statement
41:
42:For ordinals $\alpha$ and $\beta$ ([[def-ordinal]]), **exactly one** of
43:
44:$$\alpha \in \beta, \qquad \alpha = \beta, \qquad \beta \in \alpha$$
45:
46:holds. Moreover every nonempty **set** $A$ of ordinals has an $\in$-least
47:element, and consequently $A$ ordered by
48:$\alpha \le \beta :\iff \alpha \subseteq \beta$ is a well-order
49:([[def-well-order]]) whose strict part is membership.
50:
51:So the ordinals are linearly ordered by $\in$, every set of them is well
52:ordered, and [[thm-transfinite-induction]] is available on any such set. No
53:choice principle is used.
54:

