items/lem-newton-contraction-near-an-invertible-derivative.md:73:3.1 Fix $w$ in the ball, put $L:=Df(w)$, and fix $v\in\mathbb R^n$.  The map $S_v(u):=u+B(v-Lu)$ is a contraction of the complete space $\mathbb R^n$ with constant $q$, by the same estimate as step 2.1.  By [L4] it has a unique fixed point $u$, and the fixed-point equation is equivalent to $Lu=v$.  Thus $L$ is surjective.  If $Lu=0$, both $u$ and $0$ are fixed by $S_0$, so uniqueness gives $u=0$; hence $L$ is injective.  The solution map is linear by uniqueness, so it is $L^{-1}$.  [step 1.1, L4, L7]
