items/fs-strict-contraction-has-a-fixed-point.md:96:- **Compactness would repair it; boundedness would not.** On a *compact* space the strict condition does force a fixed point, by minimising $x \mapsto d(x,f(x))$; compactness of metric spaces is a later page in this library and nothing of the sort is claimed at this point. Adding **boundedness** to completeness, by contrast, is not enough, and the witness is small: on $\mathbb{N}$ put $d(m,n) := 1 + 2^{-m-1} + 2^{-n-1}$ for $m \ne n$ and $d(n,n) := 0$. Every nonzero distance lies in $(1, 7/4]$, so the triangle inequality is automatic and $d$ is a bounded metric; the space is complete because distinct points are more than $1$ apart, so a Cauchy sequence is eventually constant; and $n \mapsto n+1$ satisfies $d(m+1,n+1) = 1 + 2^{-m-2} + 2^{-n-2} < d(m,n)$ for $m \ne n$ and has no fixed point. What the present item establishes is only that completeness alone is not enough.
