items/thm-bourbaki-witt.md:78:5.1 From $f(c) \le c$ and $c \le f(c)$, antisymmetry gives $f(c) = c$, so $c$ is a fixed point of $f$. [step 4.1, step 1.2, L5] ∎
items/thm-bourbaki-witt.md:84:- Both hypotheses are load-bearing. Progressivity without chain-completeness fails ([[cex-progressive-map-without-fixed-point]]), and the fixed point is genuinely produced at the top of a chain, not by iterating $f$: no iteration argument is available, since $f$ need not be monotone and the chain $M$ need not be countable.
