    69	## Facts & Assumptions
    70	
    71	**Given:** Sets $A, A', B, B'$ and ordinals $\alpha, \beta$, in ZF. No choice principle is assumed except where the Axiom of Choice is named.
    72	
    73	[L1] A set is well-orderable if and only if it is equinumerous with an ordinal; it then has a least such ordinal $\lvert X \rvert$, which is a cardinal, and equinumerous sets receive the same one ([[lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set]], [[def-cardinal]]).
    74	
    75	[L2] A well-order is a relation that is irreflexive, transitive, trichotomous, and such that every nonempty subset has a least element ([[def-well-order]]).
    76	
    77	[L3] Every set of ordinals is well ordered by $\in$, and every nonempty set of ordinals has an $\in$-least element ([[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]]).
    78	
    79	[L4] A composition of bijections is a bijection, the inverse of a bijection is a bijection, and a function with a two-sided inverse is a bijection ([[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]).
    80	
    81	[L5] $\approx$ means that a bijection exists, and it is reflexive, symmetric and transitive ([[def-equinumerous]]).
    82	
    83	[L6] Every element of an ordinal is an ordinal and $\alpha \notin \alpha$ ([[lem-ordinal-basics]], [[def-ordinal]]).
    84	
    85	[L7] Assuming the Axiom of Choice, every set carries a well-order ([[def-axiom-of-choice]], [[thm-well-ordering-theorem]]).
    86	
    87	## Proof
    88	
    89	**Proof technique:** direct.
    90	
    91	1.1 Fix bijections $f : A \to A'$ and $g : B \to B'$; these exist by [L5], and everything below is built from them, so nothing is chosen beyond one bijection for each of the two hypotheses. [L5, given]
    92	
    93	1.2 The map $\sigma : A \sqcup B \to A' \sqcup B'$ with $\sigma(0,a) = (0, f(a))$ and $\sigma(1,b) = (1, g(b))$ has the two-sided inverse built the same way from $f^{-1}$ and $g^{-1}$, hence is a bijection. [L4, L5]
    94	
    95	1.3 The map $\pi : A \times B \to A' \times B'$, $\pi(a,b) = (f(a), g(b))$, has the two-sided inverse $(a',b') \mapsto (f^{-1}(a'), g^{-1}(b'))$, hence is a bijection. [L4, L5]
    96	
    97	1.4 The map $\Phi : {}^{B}A \to {}^{B'}A'$, $\Phi(h) = f \circ h \circ g^{-1}$, lands in ${}^{B'}A'$ and has the two-sided inverse $\Psi(h') = f^{-1} \circ h' \circ g$, since $\Psi(\Phi(h)) = f^{-1} \circ f \circ h \circ g^{-1} \circ g = h$ and symmetrically; so it is a bijection and claim (a) holds. [L4, L5]
    98	
    99	1.5 Claim (b): if $f : A \to B$ is a bijection then $S \mapsto f[S]$ maps $\mathcal{P}(A)$ to $\mathcal{P}(B)$ with two-sided inverse $T \mapsto f^{-1}[T]$, hence is a bijection. [L4, L5]
   100	
   101	1.6 On $\alpha \sqcup \beta$ define $(i,\xi) \prec (j,\eta)$ to hold when $i \in j$, or $i = j$ and $\xi \in \eta$; this is irreflexive, transitive and trichotomous by [L6] and [L3], and a nonempty $S \subseteq \alpha \sqcup \beta$ has a $\prec$-least element, namely $(0,\xi_0)$ with $\xi_0$ the $\in$-least $\xi$ having $(0,\xi) \in S$ when such a $\xi$ exists, and $(1,\eta_0)$ with $\eta_0$ the $\in$-least such $\eta$ otherwise. [L2, L3, L6]
   102	
   103	1.7 On $\alpha \times \beta$ define $(\xi,\eta) \lhd (\xi',\eta')$ to hold when $\xi \in \xi'$, or $\xi = \xi'$ and $\eta \in \eta'$; the same three properties hold by [L3] and [L6], and a nonempty $S \subseteq \alpha \times \beta$ has $\lhd$-least element $(\xi_0, \eta_0)$ where $\xi_0$ is the $\in$-least first coordinate occurring in $S$ and $\eta_0$ is the $\in$-least $\eta$ with $(\xi_0,\eta) \in S$; both are least elements of nonempty sets of ordinals, so neither is chosen. [L2, L3, L6]
   104	
